  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nRain has arrived across central and east Idaho. Low pressure \nwill move through our area today. We should see that circulation\nin the radar reflectivity fields today. Storm total rainfall \namounts remain all over the board. Models just having a tough \ntime figuring out the amount of dry air in the system verses the\nrain intensity. The NBM 10th percentile ranges from 0.10 to \n0.25 inches with slightly higher amounts across the central \nmountains into the Island Park region. The 50th percentile is \nroughly around 0.75 to 1.00 inches with higher amounts across \nthe central mountains into Island Park where the wrap around \nmoisture is expected to be more persistent. This is roughly \nwhere the official forecast is. The 90th percentile is very high\nstill, well over an inch in most places and closer to 1.50 to \n2.00 inches. So what this tells us is there is a lot of \nuncertainty with this system. Amounts will most likely range \nfrom roughly 0.50 to 1.25 inches with the highest amounts over \nthe mountains into Island Park, but it could still end up a lot \nhigher if the models have overdone the amount of dry air or it \ncould end up a lot lower if the models have not accounted for \nthe dry air enough. It will be much colder today, that we know \nfor sure! Most areas will struggle to reach 50 today. Some high \nlevel snow remains possible, mainly across the central mountains\nbut it shouldn't create too many impacts to roads, if any at \nall. \n\nThis system should exit the area by Saturday with dry weather \nsettling in for the weekend into Monday. Another low pressure system \narrives Tuesday, but qpf amounts look much less, probably less than \n0.25 inches for most of us. Temperatures will remain slightly below \naverage for most of the week, we could approach seasonable values on \nMonday ahead of the next system.",
